{"id":"cms-nh-penalty-history","title":"Federal fines on San Francisco and case homes, 2017 to 2026, from every yearly edition","question":"The current federal nursing-home file keeps about three years of fines. What do the older December editions add for San Francisco homes and the homes in the nursing-home cases?","finding":"Joining the December 2019 to 2025 editions with October 2026, 60 homes (San Francisco, the related-balance case and the homes named in other cases) carry $3.4 million of federal fines; $2.7 million of it, at 40 homes, is dated before October 2023 and so is missing from the current file. San Francisco homes: $2.1 million in all, $1.8 million before October 2023; largest Laguna Honda Hospital & Rehabilitation Ctr D/p Snf ($1.2 million, 5 fines). Largest overall: Laguna Honda Hospital & Rehabilitation Ctr D/p Snf, San Francisco ($1.2 million).","how":"Every penalty row for a California home in each yearly federal edition. An edition repeats the penalties of the editions before it while they are in its window, so for each home, penalty date and penalty type the rows of the latest edition listing them are kept (a later edition carries any amount changed on appeal). Citations are distinct survey date and regulation tag pairs across the December editions; harm is severity G to L and immediate jeopardy J to L. Homes are matched to the case list by name and city.","limits":"A fine is a federal civil money penalty; some are reduced on appeal or by waiver of a hearing, and the file gives the amount imposed. Penalties follow the building, not the operator: a fine before a change of owner belongs to the earlier operator. Citations count findings, which differ with how often a home is inspected and with complaint surveys. A name match to the case list can miss a home that renamed itself.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k":"city","t":"City","type":"text"},{"k":"inCase","t":"Why listed","type":"text"},{"k":"fines","